precision mismatch: expected f32, got f64
Blocker: nautilus#12, with chelis#902 the structural fix. `Nautilus.Special.erf`
is f32-only, so a Shoals f64 grad path cannot call it. Shoals.Pricing therefore
carries `erf64`, whose coefficients are byte-identical to the f32 original --
Abramowitz-Stegun 7.1.26, ~1.5e-7 absolute by construction of those
coefficients. Widening the cast does not widen the accuracy, so the f64 entry
point is no more accurate than the f32 one and the ~1e-6 relative ceiling on
n_cdf64 / bs_call_f64 / the Greeks follows from that, at any dtype. This is
disclosed in docs/CHELIS_SURFACE.md.

Two upstream repairs would each retire this narrowing, and they are disjoint.
nautilus#12 would grant f64 signatures, which removes the reason to duplicate
the kernel but leaves the A&S bound wherever the coefficients are used.
chelis#902 would give the language a canonical `erf` with a stated accuracy,
which removes both. Neither is a chelis arithmetic defect: f64 arithmetic is
evaluated correctly and the bound belongs to the approximation.

Measured on 0.18.6: this exact probe reports `precision mismatch: expected f32,
got f64`, i.e. the symbol resolves and refuses the width. An earlier draft of
this probe assumed `erf` was absent entirely and expected `unbound variable`;
running it is what corrected that.

On pass: determine which repair landed. If nautilus#12, replace
`Shoals.Pricing.erf64`'s body with the package `erf` and keep the accuracy rows
in docs/CHELIS_SURFACE.md, since the coefficients are unchanged. If chelis#902,
call the language `erf` and re-derive those rows from its [05-OP-N] atom's
stated bound instead of from A&S. Either way promote this probe to tests/ and
archive the docs/UPSTREAM_BUGS.md entry in the same change set.
